It is the code that's causing the error. The code is:
ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList(); 
int first = 1;
int second = 2; 
int third = 3; 

arrList.Add(first); 
// add second after first
arrList.Add(third);

// PRESS F9 to insert second after first
assertEquals(arrListCount, 3);

Please can someone help me understand why this code is causing an error and what I can do to debug it?
I have attempted to use the PexActiveFrame and the PexAssumeUnderTest where necessary to assist with the debugging, but it hasn't solved the issue. 
I'm using ReSharper as the IDE, .Net framework 4.6.1, JetBrains dotPeek and Visual Studio Test Professional 2015 for running unit tests.

A: PexContracts do not provide a way to verify asserted property states for the added code, i.e., the code after the PexAssumeUnderTest. They are meant to verify that methods do not throw any exceptions when contract conditions are fulfilled. 
Here you have:
arrList.Add(first); 
arrList.Add(second); // PRESS F9 here: Insert second after first

And then you are checking the list count and doing an assertion:
assertEquals(arrListCount, 3);

These two sections are not being verified by Pex. It works with the original 'under test' methods, not with the rest of the code. Also, Pex does not currently support adding elements with assumptions or verifying asserted properties for non-under-test calls.
You might want to consider doing this within your code, rather than within the PexContracts.
Please post this on JetBrains Pex Issues page (https://youtrack.jetbrains.com/issues/PX). They might think they can address it in a future version.
Alternatively, you can do your own verification by checking the state of your ArrayList within your original code, e.g.,
var arrListCount = arrList.Count;

// Insert second after first
arrList.Insert(1, second); 

assertEquals(arrList.Count, arrListCount + 1); 

This does give you a way of verifying the effect of arrList.Insert(1, second). Keep in mind, though, the Pex framework was designed to help find bugs in your "under test" code, while the code outside of the PexAssumeUnderTest scope is not being tested.

A: The contract provided from PEx assumes that PexAssumeUnderTest section should not contain any assertions or breakpoints, as it is meant as a "unit under test". So in your case, putting assertEquals statement outside of PexAssumeUnderTest section (which in this case it seems to be) would not be verified by PEx.
In this case, unit test (based on assumption of Pex) is checking that Insert operation will not affect ArrayList Count. If such operation would have created an ArrayList with Count not equal to 3, then Pex test (presuming that "Press F9" would have inserted second after first) would fail. 
So instead, you should do assertions inside your main part of application as shown in this example:
[Test]
public void PUT_InsertContract() 
{
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
    arrList.Add(first); // 1
    arrList.Add(third); // 3

    // PRESS F9 here: Insert second after first
    arrList.Insert(1, second);
    Assert.AreEqual(3, arrList.Count); //assert added element
}

This should give you a true test coverage of Insert method if this were an external library function.
Alternatively, you may refactor your code to be PEx-friendly or to avoid using non-member calls.
P.S. I would recommend reaching out to JetBrains directly or their community (https://www.jetbrains.com/community/platform/#qna) to discuss these. Even if you think you have found a bug, they might have had it before and they may know how to fix it.

A: As stated above, PexContracts (and PexAssumeUnderTest specifically when used in conjunction with PexInstrumentedCode) are used to ensure that the "under test" code (i.e. code in the PexAssumeUnderTest portion of the test methods) does not throw any exceptions that ad--------------------            Debug.Assert(index <= Old_arrListCount);
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == (Old_arrListContainsX ? index : -1));
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == index);
	    
	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == index); // last occurrence of x hasn't changed because the list isn't sorted and there may be duplicates
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= index || !New_arrListContainsX); 

	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);  // count increases by 1 after insertion
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX); // ensure x is contained after insertion
	    Debug.Assert(index <= New_arrListIndexOfX && New_arrListIndexOfX < Old_arrListCount + (index == Old_arrListCount)); // the new x is at index position
	    
	    IComparable comparableIndexElement = arrList[index] as IComparable;
	    if (comparableIndexElement != null)
	    {
	        Debug.Assert(comparableIndexElement.CompareTo(Old_x) == 0);
	    }
